1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,995
Coding agents can open a pull request in minutes.

2
00:00:03,944 --> 00:00:06,601
Review still takes a person.

3
00:00:06,601 --> 00:00:11,353
And the agent that wrote the change is usually the one telling you it
works.

4
00:00:12,019 --> 00:00:15,902
Countersign is a real team room where that changes.

5
00:00:15,852 --> 00:00:19,393
A teammate tried a one-line refund fix.

6
00:00:19,393 --> 00:00:22,257
It passes the test they were given.

7
00:00:22,257 --> 00:00:27,452
The verifier runs the whole suite on a clean checkout, and rejects it.

8
00:00:27,452 --> 00:00:30,655
The refund still rounds the wrong way.

9
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:34,475
The verifier does not just say no.

10
00:00:34,426 --> 00:00:38,801
It sends the failure back to the fixer with the exact output.

11
00:00:38,801 --> 00:00:43,736
The fixer starts from the rejected branch and fixes what the proof
caught.

12
00:00:44,410 --> 00:00:47,667
The second attempt passes every test.

13
00:00:47,617 --> 00:00:53,112
The new receipt is chained to the first one, so you can see what
changed between them.

14
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:59,865
An approver looks at the diff, signs the exact commit, and a real pull
request opens.

15
00:00:59,814 --> 00:01:03,370
The agent never had the authority to land it.

16
00:01:04,039 --> 00:01:08,350
Every receipt lives in the proof ledger, and in the room itself.

17
00:01:08,301 --> 00:01:12,155
What was proposed, what was proven, who signed.

18
00:01:12,819 --> 00:01:15,256
No agent ships alone.

19
00:01:15,206 --> 00:01:17,016
Countersign.

